 • “Fig. 18. Ring of Nestor. Evans’s drawing from his Knossos notebook,        
   Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, 42, pp. 82–3 (Box 10).” [31]                      
 • “Fig. 19. Ring of Nestor. CMS VI, 277.” [31]                                
 • “Fig. 20. Ring of Nestor. Watercolour painting made by E. Gilliéron fils.   
   Evans, PM III, pl. XX A.” [31]                                              
 • “The detailed stories of the find circumstances of two famous objects, the  
   Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, although told already in other        
   publications, here make compelling reading when combined with the role or   
   likely role of Emile Gilliéron fils, as interesting as the discussion of the
   iconographies themselves.” [31]                                             
 • "Reconstructed by the English archaeologist Arthur Evans between 1905 and   
   1930". [32]                                                                 
 • The excavation began "in the early spring of 1900"; after the discovery of  
   "the oldest throne in Europe," Evans first used scaffolding and then        
   wood-and-plaster columns to protect and support the remains. [32]           
 • "Inspired by the plasticity, indestructibility, and relative cheapness of   
   the material," Evans undertook the "wholesale and highly speculative        
   reconstruction" of large areas of Knossos; "By the end of 1930, modernist   
   Knossos was complete." [32]                                                 
 • "For archaeologists, Evan's reconstructions and interpretations have always 
   presented a profoundly ambiguous bequest." The source further states that   
   the concrete reconstructions cover original building stones and that Evans's
   paper reconstructions do not easily allow a perspective uncolored by the    
   prejudices of Evans and his team. [32]                                      
 • The source says that Evans's methods were distinguished by "a delirious     
   interpretative incontinence that seemed to owe more to spiritualism than to 
   science" and that his self-fashioning as an archaeological prophet and      
   magician was correspondingly grandiose. [32]                                
 • The title page identifies the work as Cathy Gere's Knossos and the Prophets 
   of Modernism, published by the University of Chicago Press in 2009; the     
   cataloging information lists Arthur Evans and the Palace of Knossos among   
   its subjects. [32]                                                          
 • The Ring of Nestor's authenticity remains unresolved in the supplied        
   scholarly material: it is treated as a gemma dubitanda whose Bronze Age     
   affiliation and genuineness cannot be decisively established. [30]          
 • Athenian archaeological-board members reportedly rejected the Ring of Nestor
   as a modern forgery, whereas Arthur Evans later privately purchased and     
   published it; this establishes competing historical positions but does not  
   by itself prove either the forgery or authenticity claim. [30]              
 • The principal evidentiary weakness cited against treating the Ring of Nestor
   as authentic Bronze Age evidence is the lack of decisive provenance from a  
   professional, well-documented excavation; its reported discovery at         
   Kakovatos near Pylos remains explicitly alleged. [30]                       


